        "공",
        "연",
        "말"
      ],
      "number_guard": [
        "몇",
        "한",
        "두",
        "세",
        "네",
        "다섯",
        "여섯",
        "일곱",
        "여덟",
        "아홉",
        "열",
        "여러",
        "몇몇"
      ]
    },
    "ascii": {
      "_comment": "`on` and `off` are both the ON/OFF polarity words and the preposition in \"on the right\".  Without these exclusions \"Turn detection off on the right\" resolves to true and false at the same time and the command is lost to ambiguity.",
      "not_followed_by": {
        "on": [
          " the left",
          " the right",
          " left",
          " right",
          " the camera",
          " camera",
          " the screen",
          " screen"
        ],
        "off": [
          " the left",
          " the right"
        ]
      }
    },
    "list_items": {
      "_comment": "Class-list guard (command_normalizer.py): in an 'only these objects' context (a class is named, or 만 / only is used), a word joined into the list (X하고, X이랑, X랑, X과, X와, X만; or English X and Y, X, Y) must be a known phrase. An unknown one is an unresolved class term: the command is never run as a shorter list and no class is guessed. These words are fillers, not class candidates.",
